---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DocsSidebar from "../components/DocsSidebar.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import IconSetsDemo from "../components/demos/IconSetsDemo";

const reactSnippet = `import { Badge, Button } from "@kernelui-lib/react";
import { Check, ArrowRight } from "lucide-react";

<Button variant="primary" iconStart={<Check />}>Done</Button>
<Button aria-label="Next" iconStart={<ArrowRight />} />   {/* icon-only */}
<Badge variant="success"><Check /> Shipped</Badge>`;

const elementsSnippet = `<kernel-button variant="primary">
  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true">…</svg>
  Done
</kernel-button>

<kernel-badge variant="success">
  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true">…</svg> Shipped
</kernel-badge>

<!-- Any other icon element: mark it data-icon -->
<kernel-badge><my-icon name="check" data-icon></my-icon> Shipped</kernel-badge>`;

const tokensSnippet = `:root {
  --kernel-icon-size: 1.2em;        /* drawn size inside the 1lh slot */
  --kernel-icon-stroke-width: 1.75; /* outline sets only, in viewBox units */
}`;
---

<BaseLayout title="Icons" description="Use any icon set with Kernel: icons drop into a concentric slot, and two tokens tune a whole set to match Kernel's type." withSidebar>
  <DocsSidebar slot="sidebar" />
  <div class="docs-prose">
    <h1>Icons</h1>
    <p>
      Kernel doesn't ship an icon set, and doesn't need an icon prop on
      most components. Pass any <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code>,
      <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> or icon component from the set you already
      use. Badges, buttons, tags and suggestions find an icon at either end
      and give it a slot that's concentric with the rounded corner. An
      icon-only button comes out square, or a circle at Round.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-example docs-example--stack">
      <IconSetsDemo client:visible />
    </div>

    <h2>Two tokens per set</h2>
    <p>
      Icon sets draw their glyphs at different sizes inside their viewBox,
      and with different stroke weights, so the same <code>1em</code> icon
      looks bigger or bolder from one set to the next. Set these once, on
      <code>:root</code>, for whichever set you use. The demo above shows
      tuned values for five popular sets.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={tokensSnippet} lang="css" />
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>--kernel-icon-size</code> is the size the icon is drawn at
        inside its slot. The slot itself is one line tall, so this can't
        make icons push a control taller. The default is
        <code>1.25em</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--kernel-icon-stroke-width</code> overrides an outline set's
        stroke, in that set's own viewBox units. The default,
        <code>auto</code>, leaves each icon's stroke alone. It needs
        container style queries (Chrome 111+, Safari 18+); elsewhere icons
        keep their own stroke. Filled sets such as Phosphor and Radix Icons
        have no stroke to change.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      To tune your own set, put an icon next to a capital letter in a
      Badge and a Button, then adjust the size until the glyph is a little
      taller than the capitals, and the stroke until it matches the
      weight of the text.
    </p>

    <h2>React</h2>
    <CodeBlock code={reactSnippet} lang="tsx" />
    <p>
      An <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> or any
      component counts as an icon. A plain element such as
      <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> counts as text, so wrap a component that
      renders text in a <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>. Give an icon-only Button
      an <code>aria-label</code>. For an icon-only Badge, label the icon
      itself with <code>role="img"</code> and <code>aria-label</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Web Components</h2>
    <CodeBlock code={elementsSnippet} lang="html" />
    <p>
      An <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> or
      <code>&lt;picture&gt;</code> first or last among the children is an
      icon. Mark any other icon element, such as an icon custom element,
      with <code>data-icon</code>. Badges keep this up to date if their
      children change later.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
